<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Washington Williams</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1849">1849</a>–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> Williams was an African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> veteran, minister, lawyer and historian, author of a pioneering History of the Negro Race in America. After visiting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> in 1890 he wrote an Open Letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> denouncing the abuses he saw, one of the first public exposés of the regime. He died in Blackpool, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in 1891.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-washington-williams.jpg" alt="George Washington Williams" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harper &amp; Brothers (publisher) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_W._Williams_from_History_of_Negro_Troops.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
